Compose every frame with a self-consistent async-scroll snapshot
(WEBKIT_COMPOSITE_SCROLL_SYNC, default on; =0 disables).

With async (threaded) scrolling, the scrolling thread first moves the
scrolled content (boundsOrigin) and then walks related nodes to compensate
position:fixed/sticky layers. Composites the scrolling thread requests
itself are consistent (the request is deferred until the walk finishes,
LayerTreeHost::m_compositionRequiredInScrollingThread), but composites
triggered by anything else — tile-paint completions, main-thread rendering
updates, video, animations — flush layer state under per-layer locks only,
with no synchronization against that walk. A composite landing mid-walk
renders the new scroll offset against a stale sticky/fixed compensation;
a desynchronized main thread can also commit sticky positions computed
from a scroll offset several frames old (GraphicsLayerCoordinated::
updateGeometry). Either way sticky navbars visibly lag behind fast flicks
and snap back, with the error proportional to per-frame scroll delta.

Fix: before flushing layer state for a composite, take the scrolling tree
lock, re-apply the tree's layer positions, and hold the lock across the
whole flush. Every composed frame then carries an atomic snapshot of
scroll offset + dependent fixed/sticky layer positions. The tree reference
reaches the compositor via LayerTreeHost::updateRendering (thread-safe
RefPtr handoff under m_scrollingTreeLock).

